# 0 title
  0 en title | Talking Machines: A Short History.

# 1 heading
  1 en heading | Talking Machines.

# 2 paragraph
  2 en | By J. R. R. Smith, Ph.D. — 12 March 2024

# 3 paragraph
  3 en | People have tried to build machines that speak for more than 250 years.
  4 en | In 1779, Christian Kratzenstein built resonators that produced the five long vowels (in IPA: [aː], [eː], [iː], [oː] and [uː]).
  5 en | Wolfgang von Kempelen's "acoustic-mechanical speech machine" followed in 1791.

# 4 heading
  6 en heading | Early electronic systems.

# 5 paragraph
  7 en | Bell Labs demonstrated the Voder at the 1939 New York World's Fair.
  8 en | An operator played it like an organ — with a wrist bar, ten keys and a foot pedal — and it took about a year to learn.
  9 en | Dr. Homer Dudley, its inventor, also built the vocoder (see Fig. 3).

# 6 quote
  10 en | "The Voder was a curiosity; the vocoder was the real invention."
  11 en | — an imaginary historian, in A Made-Up History of Sound (p. 42)

# 7 paragraph
  12 en | In the 1960s, John Larry Kelly Jr. and Louis Gerstman used an IBM 704 to sing "Daisy Bell".
  13 en | Arthur C. Clarke saw the demo at Bell Labs and used the idea in 2001: A Space Odyssey.

# 8 heading
  14 en heading | The 1970s and 1980s.

# 9 listItem
  15 en | Texas Instruments' Speak & Spell (1978) used linear predictive coding.

# 10 listItem
  16 en | DECtalk (1984) grew out of Dennis Klatt's research.

# 11 listItem
  17 en | The MITalk system, i.e. the work of Allen, Hunnicutt and Klatt, became a book.

# 12 paragraph
  18 en | Imagine a speech chip that cost $50 in 1980 and less than $5 by 1990.
  19 en | Say version v1.2.3 of an engine runs on a phone at 3.14 times real time.

# 13 heading
  20 en heading | Formants and concatenation.

# 14 paragraph
  21 en | A formant synthesizer computes the spectrum from rules; the transfer function of a simple resonator is , which we will not need here.
  22 en | Concatenative systems instead glue recorded units together:

# 15 paragraph
  23 en | Unit selection, popular in the 2000s, picks the best sequence from a large database of recorded speech; see the overview for details.

# 16 heading
  24 en heading | Neural voices.

# 17 paragraph
  25 en | Modern systems such as WaveNet (2016) and Tacotron produce speech that is hard to tell from a person.
  26 en | Open models now run offline: Kokoro has 82 million parameters, and a reader can use it for both Chinese and English.

# 18 paragraph
  27 en | Reading Chinese inside English text needs care.
  28 en | The writer Lu Xun (Chinese:
  29 zh | 鲁迅
  30 en | ; 1881–1936) is a good test, as is the game Pokémon (Japanese:
  31 -- | ポケモン
  32 en | A good reader says the Chinese name in Chinese and skips the Japanese one.

# 19 paragraph
  33 en | Some sentences are long, and a reader has to split them somewhere sensible, for example at a comma, a semicolon or a dash, because a sentence that goes on and on without a real pause — like this one,
  34 en | which keeps adding clauses about vowels, resonators, organs, wrist bars, foot pedals, speech chips and phones — would otherwise take far too long before the first sound comes out of the speaker.

